 time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a91fb75-0041-5c82-8578-4977beec3c1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357699 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n6{y}^{″}=-18y+9{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357700 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357700 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357700 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357700 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=16\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357700 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a59a904-03e2-5485-b6c2-876a20fc36bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357700 #edb/ref 1074 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n8{y}^{″}=-16{y}^{′}-10y+4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392864 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392864 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392864 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186392866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392864 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2118b5c2-d188-5b07-bc58-c6425a8b5a57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392865 #edb/ref 1080 "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡1+3)\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392865 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392865 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d69d6c7b-6c0f-5833-b358-024e1b2373a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392866 #edb/ref 1080 "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392866 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392866 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "141a0a74-e302-523c-9f8c-bad2145b337b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392867 #edb/ref 1080 "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-2)\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392867 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392867 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc71a800-3396-5d41-b999-dc1cc70c1cd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392868 #edb/ref 1080 "y(1)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2+3)\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392868 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392868 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d183e142-084c-5c40-a40a-3e43c9bd291f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392869 #edb/ref 1080 "y(1)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3-1)\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392869 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392869 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d2b2af7-c2a5-5791-bb97-7ee22ccd83a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404686 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404686 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404686 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404686 #edb/ref 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in centimeters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404686 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f3b61bf-2d9c-5a4f-b827-01f0e0d4bec0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404686 #edb/ref 1074 "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=49\\,⟹\\,\\omega =7.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,\\beta $ are constants to be determined.\n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2},\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{2})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404687 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404687 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404687 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186404692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404687 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "039eefc7-4800-59f4-a9af-2eb142d7c4e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404688 #edb/ref 1080 "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1\\,\\text{cm}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404688 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404688 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "25213c17-424a-52a3-9371-0722697b6bae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404689 #edb/ref 1080 "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2\\,\\text{cm}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404689 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 175921860